# Two-factor authentication backend TODO

Generated by the light-it auth package. `auth:setup` wrote every 2FA stub,
action and route, but it cannot edit your boilerplate's own login or your own
`User` model - neither of those two files is generated by this package, so
nothing here rewrites them automatically.

## Two manual steps

### 1. The challenge action

Open `LoginAction` and inject the challenge action through its constructor:

```php
{{gateConstructorSnippet}}
```

Then, right after the host login's `$request->session()->regenerate();` and
before `return $user;`, call it:

```php
{{gateCallSnippet}}
```

The challenge action tears the just-created session back down itself before
throwing a challenge, so the call point only matters in that it must run
after `attempt()`/`regenerate()` has committed the session and before the
user is returned - anywhere in that window works.

Every alternative login path this package generates (OTP today; Google SSO
and passkeys as they land) already routes through the same challenge action
via `LoginByUserAction::execute()`. This is the only manual step left to run
your own native login through the same challenge.

Once wired in, a user with 2FA configured gets a `200` with
`token_type: "verification_required"` (or `"setup_required"` if 2FA is
mandatory and they haven't set it up yet) instead of a session on login.
Completing the challenge - the one-time password or a recovery code -
creates the session and returns the same `UserResource` a normal login does.

Each challenge token only completes the endpoint its reason was issued for:
a `setup_required` or `verification_required` token both unlock
`/2fa/complete` - the same token used for `/2fa/setup` works to confirm
enrollment right after - but only a `verification_required` token unlocks
`/2fa/verify-recovery-code`. The token is valid until its TTL expires; that
TTL is `google2fa.challenge_ttl_minutes` in `config/google2fa.php`.

With `google2fa.mandatory=false`, the challenge action only ever challenges a
user who has already set 2FA up (see
`IssueTwoFactorChallengeAction::execute()`); it never hands a
`setup_required` token to a logged-in user who hasn't enrolled yet. That user
enables 2FA from their account instead, over the cookie session
(`auth:sanctum`, no challenge token):

- `GET /2fa/status` returns `{ available, enabled, mandatory }` - the
  boilerplate's `UserResource` has no 2FA flag, and this package never edits
  it. `available` is `google2fa.enabled`.
- `POST /2fa/enable` with `{ password }` stores a new secret and returns
  `{ qr, secret }`. It answers `409` (`2fa_already_configured`) when 2FA is
  already on, so an active secret is never overwritten.
- `POST /2fa/confirm` with `{ one_time_password }` checks the first code,
  sets `two_factor_auth_activated_at` and returns `{ recovery_codes }`. It
  answers `409` when 2FA is already on or `enable` was never called.
- Both answer `409` (`2fa_unavailable`) while `google2fa.enabled` is `false`,
  and both re-check and write the user row under `lockForUpdate()`.
  `confirm` counts the attempt and checks the code before that
  transaction, so a `database` cache store can't roll a failed attempt back.

2FA counts as on when the user has a secret and `two_factor_auth_activated_at`
(`hasTwoFactorAuthenticationConfigured()`, the check the login gate uses).
`disable` and `regenerate-recovery-codes` answer `409`
(`cannot_disable_2fa_unconfigured` / `cannot_regenerate_2fa_unconfigured`)
when it is not on.

`disable` and `regenerate-recovery-codes` take `{ password, code }`: the
password and proof of the second factor. A 6-digit `code` is checked as a
live one-time password (claimed once, like at login); anything else as a
recovery code, which is spent. A wrong code answers `422` (`invalid_otp`)
and counts toward the per-user lockout, counted outside the transaction
that spends a recovery code.

A wrong password on `enable`, `disable`, `regenerate-recovery-codes` or
`request-reset` answers `422` (`invalid_password`), not `401`: the user is
still signed in, and the frontend sends every `401` back to the login screen.

A one-time password is accepted once per user: a second request with the same
code inside its validity window answers `422` (`otp_already_used`), including
a login `complete` right after `confirm` accepted that code. The claim lives in
the cache (`Cache::add()`), so use a store shared by every app server, not
`array`.

`routes/two-factor-auth.php` throttles every route except `status` with
`throttle:2fa`: 5 requests a minute per signed-in user on the account routes
(`enable`, `confirm`, `disable`, `regenerate-recovery-codes` and
`request-reset` share that bucket), per challenge token on the challenge
routes, plus 30 a minute per IP. `complete`, `verify-recovery-code`, `confirm`,
`disable` and `regenerate-recovery-codes` also count every code attempt
against the per-user lockout. `lightit-auth-laravel`'s
own service provider registers that named limiter in `boot()` when
`\Lightit\Authentication\Domain\TwoFactorRateLimiter` exists - no
`AppServiceProvider` paste needed, and it still runs under `php artisan
route:cache`, unlike a file-scope call in the routes file would. This is why
`lightit-auth-laravel` must stay a runtime dependency (`composer require`,
never `--dev`): its provider needs to boot in production for the limiter to
be registered at all.

### 2. Extend `TwoFactorAuthenticatable`

`\Lightit\Users\Domain\Models\User` must extend
`\Lightit\Authentication\Domain\TwoFactorAuthenticatable` instead of
`Illuminate\Foundation\Auth\User`. Every stub above calls methods
(`hasTwoFactorAuthenticationConfigured()`, `getRecoveryCodes()`, ...) that
only exist on that base class - without this change, `IssueTwoFactorChallengeAction`
never recognizes your User as one it can challenge, so it throws a
`LogicException` on every login instead of ever challenging anyone.

Delete this file once you've made both changes.

### Optional: avoid the `Login` event side effect

`$guard->attempt()` fires Laravel's `Login` event even for a user who is about
to be challenged. If your listeners on that event should only run for a fully
authenticated session, swap `attempt()` for `validate()` + the challenge
action + `login()`:

```php
if (! $request->hasSession()) {
    throw new UnauthenticatedException;
}

if (! $guard->validate(['email' => $credentials->email, 'password' => $credentials->password])) {
    throw new UnauthenticatedException;
}

/** @var User $user */
$user = $guard->getLastAttempted();

$this->issueTwoFactorChallengeAction->execute($user);

$guard->login($user);
$request->session()->regenerate();
```

`validate()` checks the credentials without logging the user in, so the
challenge action runs first and the session is only ever established once
the challenge (if any) is cleared - `login()` here does what `attempt()`
used to, and `regenerate()` still runs after it, exactly as it does on the
path this optional snippet replaces.
